  "summary": {
    "as": "Introduced in House",
    "asOf": "2026-09-24",
    "date": "2026-10-06T14:19:57Z",
    "text": "Motion Picture, Television, and Entertainment Revitalization Act\n\nThis bill establishes a federal business tax credit for a percentage of the compensation paid for services performed in the United States related to a qualified film or television production. (Conditions and exceptions apply.)\n\nThe bill defines a qualified film or television production as any production that is\n\na feature film, television pilot, or television season;produced in the ordinary course of business for commercial purposes;capitalized;completed during the tax year with more than $1 million in total costs; andfilmed in the United States for 75% of the photography days.Under the bill, various types of programming are not a qualified film or television production, including news programs, live sporting events, and programming intended primarily for social media platforms.\n\nThe amount of the tax credit is 20% (base tax credit percentage) of the compensation paid for services performed in the United States related to a qualified film or television production. The base tax credit percentage increases by increments of 5% (up to a maximum tax credit percentage of 30%) if\n\nat least 30% of the production is filmed in a federally declared disaster area or rural qualified opportunity zone,the production is independently produced, orthe production meets certain multi-state or increased domestic production requirements.Special rules apply to animations, visual effects productions, and post-production activities.\n\nFinally, the tax credit may be transferred to an unrelated third party in exchange for cash."
  },
